Planning and interpreting XPS — Baer and colleagues (@cm-xps-guide-source: 0, is #reference, #part-of @cm-sources) { r_6CY69TZ791W5PW0SZCY536SVH5
Donald R. Baer and colleagues. *Practical guides for x-ray photoelectron spectroscopy: First steps in planning, conducting, and reporting XPS measurements*, Journal of Vacuum Science & Technology A 37, 031401 (2019), DOI 10.1116/1.5065501. [Institutional catalogue](https://www.nist.gov/publications/practical-guides-x-ray-photoelectron-spectroscopy-first-steps-planning-conducting-and) · [Thirty-five-page author manuscript](https://tsapps.nist.gov/publication/get_pdf.cfm?pub_id=926498) · [Published article](https://doi.org/10.1116/1.5065501).

Read the complete discussion of selecting an observation, sample history, surface sensitivity, interference, fitting and reporting. Use the spectrum examples to distinguish a physically supported interpretation from a merely good numerical fit.

The author list, introduction and selected sample/measurement-planning passages on manuscript pages 10–15 were inspected. The complete figures, fitting appendix and experiments were not independently reproduced. The institutional catalogue orders authors differently from the manuscript; the manuscript supplies the author ordering used here. Free institutional reading was verified; an unrestricted adaptation licence was not established. The source remains external.

